<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baghdad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baghdad is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, founded in 762 by the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mansur as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. It became one of the largest cities in the world and a great centre of trade and learning. It came under Seljuk control in 1055 and was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1258, after which it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and was later contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdad.jpg" alt="Baghdad" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USACE HQ, JIM GORDAN, CIV, USACE · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5628442718_b10fc2c47f_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The word 'algebra' comes from al-jabr in the title of al-Khwarizmi's treatise on calculation, written in Abbasid <b>Baghdad</b> around 820.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aghlabids adopted the Mu'tazilite rationalist doctrine after the caliph al-Ma'mun (813–833) made it official in <b>Baghdad</b>, and imposed it as the state doctrine of Ifriqiya, though the Maliki majority rejected its teaching that the Qur'an was created rather than God's eternal word.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Yusuf ibn Tashfin took the title Amir al-Muslimin, Commander of the Muslims, deliberately stopping short of the caliphal title and acknowledging the nominal supremacy of the Abbasid caliph in <b>Baghdad</b> while exercising independent sovereign authority over his domains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Aq Qoyunlu partisans held <b>Baghdad</b> until 1508 and Murad died in exile some years later, while Diyarbakır passed to the Safavids and then, after the Ottoman victory at the Battle of Chaldiran in 1514, to the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two years later, as the last Fatimid caliph al-Adid lay dying in September 1171, Saladin had the Friday sermon pronounced in the name of the Abbasid caliph of <b>Baghdad</b>, ending the Shi'a Fatimid Caliphate and returning Egypt to Sunni allegiance; the Ayyubid state dates from this moment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Mardavij was murdered in 935, Hasan fought for years to secure Ray, Isfahan and the Jibal, while Ahmad advanced into Khuzistan and, in December 945, entered <b>Baghdad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
